        "date": "2026-11-12T05:00:31Z"
      },
      "message": "feat(fixtures): a rotary subplate on the plate grid raises the A centerline for the index swing\n\nA rotary subplate (#821, as corrected 2026-11-11: a base plate the 4th\naxis is bolted to, not a plate turning with A) is a built-in accessory\n(`rotary_subplate`): stated length, width, thickness and two pin bores,\ngeneric default figures. It lands fixed and is placed on a fixture\nplate's dowel pair by its pins with PlaceOnPlate - a single hole or a\npair at another spacing is refused by name (also on a plate edit or a\nresize of the placed subplate).\n\nThe index-swing clause now judges with the EFFECTIVE nose_to_axis: the\nsetup's stated figure (SetSetupNoseToAxis, MCP set_setup_nose_to_axis,\nthe setup's Machine page) when it has one, else the machine's less the\ntallest subplate stack in the setup (the subplate plus every plate under\nit). A stack of unknown thickness (an imported plate) or an effective\nfigure at or under zero refuses the swing by name. No subplate: judged\nword for word as before. MCP get_rotary_stack reads it back. Setup key\nnose_to_axis and the accessory kind are additive; old files load\nunchanged.\n\nRefs #821\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
